r two months later it seized  power by force  provoking widespread criticism  suchi's previous detention did little to  undermine her popularity  but now it's unclear how active and  vocal  her supporters will be under threat of  military action  laura about manly al jazeera  not speak to david matheson who's a  yangon based analyst of myanmar he's  joining us from chiang mai in thailand  thanks for speaking to us on the  newshour so we have a situation now  where the military  is saying that it will hold on to power  for one year and then it will pass it on  to the civilian government  how likely is that  well whenever militaries stage a coup  and set a time limit  um i think it's fair to say don't trust  them um once they get a taste of power  i i don't think whatever time frame um  that they commit to can actually be  trusted  um you know and all of these promises  that we're seeing coming out today that  they want to hold another election  and and and all of these things this is  fundamentally a naked grab at power  and i don't think that the commander and  chief and those close to him can be  trusted anymore to actually  so what do you think their longer game  plan is  i i think holding on to power and and  basically shifting things  um to maintain their interests now  conventional wisdom  last week was that they've got it so  good anyway why would they actually  stage occur  um well obviously their calculations are  a much different i think that they can  better serve their interests um  holding on to power like this and um and  unfortunately the democratic progress of  the country will suffer  i mean that's the thing you talk about  their interest but they already had  um significant power and they were  looking after their interests in myanmar  so  just a few days ago they also appeared  to walk back threats of a coup  so why do you think they did this now  um i think it's it's it's essentially  about one man it's about the commander  achievement online  and he had no uh um he had no retirement  plan  he wanted to hold on to power and he  decided to take the entire institution  of  the military with it and he's been  setting up i think for several months  this very careful narrative  um that there was something deeply wrong  with the elections which is just not  true  um you know there were certainly  deficiencies but but the extent of  of the uh the malfeasance that the  military is is alleging  it's just not true i think this is all  about um the deluded grandeur of  one man and the fact that the military  thinks that it can actually advance its  interest  by holding on to you know all the levers  of power and unfortunately i think  that's going to be counterproductive  both for them but definitely for the  rest of the country what do you think  will happen on the ground because the  aung san tsuchi  the nld leader has called for protest do  you think people will  actually come out in protest  i think if you compare some of the  public um  outpouring of support for aung san  tsuchi and the nld ahead of the election  and the the jubilation over the the  landslide victory  um this is essentially a betrayal of all  of those people who voted for hong kong  sochi  so i i do expect some backlash people  will be furious  throughout the country i mean there is  no way that the military can argue that  this was a close election  that there was um uh graft and fraud in  involved um which could question the  outcome  and so people will quite rightly see  this as  as a grab for power and that then the  military saw losers  um and and basically want to take over  the country again this is a massive  leap backwards and and people have been  used to their freedoms over the past 10  years  and the military is directly challenging  the people it's not just an elite grab  of power  this is the military challenging most of  the people of myanmar what do you think  the range of options will be for the  international community to conside